WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
two u.s navy ships have sailed through  the taiwan strait in a passage the white  house described as being consistent with  international law the sailing comes just  weeks after the visit of u.s house  speaker nancy pelosi to taiwan  china objected to the visit and held its  largest ever military drills around the  island to which taiwan responded with  its own set of exercises  all this has brought into sharp relief  the question of how taiwan defends  itself against any possible chinese  invasion  dw's zachary lee and cherry chan report  robin she loves military hardware he's a  tour guide by day but for the past two  decades he spent his spare time taking  pictures of military exercises  when taiwan responded to chinese drills  earlier this month with miso tests of  its own  she was right there filming  [Music]  i got here more than two hours before  the announced time of the tests i was  worried that many people would come i  wanted to get the best spot  as a former navi radar operator she also  listens to radio message and tracks  movement by both the taiwanese and  chinese armed forces around the island  he says taiwan has not done enough to  improve its military strength  we have never wanted to attack anyone  we are only developing weapons for  self-defense  we should do more of that  so he is happy to see that here in  pindon county a weapons manufacturer is  carrying out missile and rocket tests  taiwan faces unprecedented provocation  from china and now it is much more eager  to develop its own missiles  at this military base behind me a  taiwanese weapon developer is conducting  a series of live fire missile tests this  next month  they are held here regularly but with  escalating threats from china  they carry more significance than ever  military experts say taiwan needs to  establish its own defense strategy but  now one based merely on how many weapons  it has  woman  we must get better at asymmetric warfare  if we try to compete on the number of  weapons we will be dragged into an arms  race  that would be really bad for us  he says taiwan has tried to move away  from buying weapons from just one  country the u.s  but that has proved almost impossible  many countries have cited a variety of  reasons for refusing to sell arms to  taiwan  we have always wanted to acquire  submarines from germany  for robinshi the message is clear  taiwan needs to be more confident in  developing its own defensive  capabilities only land can stay up to  the threat from abroad  and norway is that threat felt more  acutely perhaps than taiwan's jinmen  island which lies less than five  kilometers from china's coastline and  dw's chief international editor richard  walker joins me now from june man  richard from where you are give us a  sense of the scale of the threat that  taiwan's facing  yeah well biresh there really is a sense  at the moment that the the threat from  china is as big as it's ever been um and  there's a sense of a potential kind of  moment of truth approaching down the  line but just to give you a little bit  of scene setting first of all of where i  am here i'm on an island actually called  little jin men which is off jin man this  cluster of islands uh controlled by  taiwan very close to the chinese  mainland and if you just look behind me  across the bay here through the haze you  can see the chinese city of shaman  across the water  less than five kilometers just like you  said and it really it it feels so close  and these islands jin men they're no  stranger to the china threat so at the  very end  of the civil war in 1949 the pla  were  landing on these shores trying to take  these islands at the very end of the  civil war and you still see uh these  spikes uh these defensive spikes which  are against uh uh landings taking place  here uh which are dotted all around  these islands everywhere so these  islands really exude this sort of  history of the conflict between china  and taiwan  but just very recently during the  chinese exercises that took place to  complain about the visit of u.s speaker  nancy pelosi  one of these islands just the next one  along was buzzed by a chinese drone and  the chinese released footage from that  drone which showed taiwanese soldiers on  the ground throwing rocks at this drone  to try to shoot away and this really  brought home some of the problems that  taiwan faces at the moment the sheer  proximity of china but also the fact  that all they had to to show away these  drones were rocks so the chinese  government has been rushing now uh to  say that they're going to secure for  next year all sorts of anti-drone  technologies but but really a kind of a  little palpable and rather embarrassing  reminder there of the threat  and obviously leads to the larger  question of what is taiwan's strategy to  defend itself from a much bigger  neighbor  yeah well i mean this is really an  ongoing debate that's been heightened  this year not just because of the pelosi  visit but also because of the ukraine  war uh that's been really something of a  wake up call not just for taiwanese  politicians but taiwanese military but  also ordinary time and his people  thinking gosh if it can happen there it  can happen here and this is added to the  debate and we heard referred to just in  the report there about asymmetric  warfare the fact that taiwan will never  be able to match up with the chinese in  terms of numbers or in terms of military  budget so that it needs to be smart  about using its limited military budget  to buy the kind of weapons that it might  be able to use to fight a bit like the  ukrainians in in an invasion scenario  but even that is controversial with some  people saying well you can't just  go for the sort of weapons that allow  you to fight back once someone's invaded  you also want to deter that invasion so  really what you need is a sort of  balance between big ticket military  items and the smaller scale ones but  this does bring us also to a certain  amount of tension between taiwan and the  united states with the sense that i've  been getting from some people talking to  i've been talking to privately here in  taiwan that they get the sense that the  americans still been dragging their feet  in providing some of the weapons that  they really need so all is not  necessarily particularly well also in  the relationship between the us and  taiwan  in dealing with this threat from china  you earlier spoke of a moment of truth  approaching richard has that  moment of truth arrived there because of  nancy spear nancy pelosi's recent visit  well i i don't think so i mean there are  all sorts of different takes on nancy  pelosi's visit and what the chinese  exercises that took place after it mean  um some people i've been talking to here  say that they do feel that the chinese  did show a degree of restraint in those  exercises but what they also did was  establish what's been talked about as a  new normal the chinese  fighter jets for instance now go further  than they did across what's called the  median line at the line sort of in  between taiwan and china  on the in the taiwan strait but the kind  of moment of truth that i'm really  talking about here is the fact that  china says that it wants to reunify as  they put it with taiwan and a great  majority of the people of taiwan say  that they simply don't want that how do  you resolve that then without going to  war  richard walker  lovely talking to you thank you so much  for joining us from genman island  and tensions over taiwan are having an  impact on other countries in the region  as well japan for instance  its westernmost island yonaguni lies  just 108 kilometers east of taiwan it is  in fact closer to taipei than it is to  tokyo and that has its 1500 residents  worried it could become a new front line  if hostilities break out in the future  fisherman ishinari toshitsugu makes  everything ship shape for his next big  catch he needs one urgently because he  hasn't been able to fish as usual  after the recent chinese military drills  around taiwan the 32 year old was banned  from heading out to sea for five days it  was deemed too dangerous because of the  proximity to the contested island nearby  the rockets fell into the sea just 80  kilometers from here we could hear it  happen and how did the government in  tokyo react with a silly piece of paper  they sent us a fax to inform us about it  what am i to make of that  the island of yonaguni is some two  thousand kilometers from tokyo but it  feels like half a world away  fishing and pony breeding are the  economic lifeblood of the island which  rarely receives visitors  our cameras are a cause of interest  straight  but i recently started worrying about  the future  the future of the island or rather the  protection of it has traditionally been  entrusted to the so-called shisa  mythical guardians made of stone that  keep evil spirits away  but the mayor isn't happy to rely on  them for protection against china  he wants to be able to evacuate the  island in the event of an attack which  is a challenge considering the small  harbor and short runway  it's all about saving lives  it's crucial to avoid a scenario like in  ukraine  where civilians and soldiers are bundled  up together  [Music]  the island is home to a radar station  and 160 defense troops have taken up  residence to monitor the airspace and  the sea  but nobody on the island really believes  that they can prevent a potential attack  by china  in the japanese parliament in tokyo  he understands the concerns of  yonaguni's residence  but says it's important to look at the  big picture  the whole area is a battlefield he says  not just taiwan  all of this area  it's not just about yonaguni but also  about the hundreds of thousands of  residents of the other islands in the  vicinity about the evacuation of the  taiwanese japanese americans and  europeans who live there  we also have the difficult task of  improving our defense capabilities in  the region  in the meantime the fishermen of  yonaguni are still waiting for their  next big catch  hoping that peace prevails on the island  and beyond

